<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Minimal. Sign with -entitlements entitlements.plist, or let the signer
	     derive them from the provisioning profile.

	     What is deliberately NOT here, and why:

	     get-task-allow
	       This used to be <true/>, with a comment claiming dyld needs it to map
	       the injected dylib. That is false: get-task-allow only permits
	       debugging (ptrace / task_for_pid on our own task). An LC_LOAD_DYLIB is
	       resolved by dyld whether or not the app is debuggable.
	       Meanwhile it is the single cheapest integrity signal a game can read
	       about itself: one call to SecTaskCopyValueForEntitlement and the app
	       knows it was signed for development. Removed at signing time, and
	       Core/Integrity.m also stops answering true at runtime for builds that
	       still carry it.

	     application-identifier / keychain-access-groups / team-identifier
	       These must match the certificate's Team ID and the bundle id the
	       profile actually allows. A hardcoded TEAMID placeholder here is worse
	       than absent: it either breaks signing or produces a mismatched
	       identity. Signers derive all three from the profile.

	     com.apple.private.security.no-container / platform-application
	       Private, jailbreak-class entitlements. They cannot be signed into a
	       normal sideload, and asking for them makes the build look worse, not
	       better. They live in entitlements.trollstore.plist, which is only for
	       an install that can actually grant them. -->

	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
